      content: 'A 38-year-old right-handed male high school mathematics teacher presents to the PM&R clinic referred by his PCP for evaluation of involuntary movements and cognitive concerns. He reports 8 months of progressive involuntary jerking movements in his arms and face that worsen with stress, increasing difficulty with lesson planning and grading that used to take him half the time, irritability and impulsive outbursts that have strained his marriage, and two recent falls when turning quickly in his classroom. His father was diagnosed with Huntington disease at age 42 and died at age 55. The patient has never undergone genetic testing. He is accompanied by his wife, who separately tells you he was nearly fired last semester for erratic behavior in the classroom. They have an 18-year-old daughter about to start college and a 15-year-old son. The patient states: "I know what this probably is. My father had the same thing. But I need to hear it from a doctor before I can deal with it."',
