    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Movement disorder characterization**: Chorea — involuntary, non-repetitive, purposeless movements that flow from one body part to another. Distinguish from dystonia, tics, myoclonus, or tremor. Distribution (face, trunk, extremities), severity, impact on function, worsening factors (stress, fatigue, anxiety).\n- **Cognitive symptoms**: Executive dysfunction (planning, organizing, multitasking), processing speed decline, difficulty with mental flexibility. These are often the earliest and most disabling cognitive changes in HD and can precede motor symptoms by years. Assess impact on work performance.\n- **Psychiatric symptoms**: Depression (prevalence 30-50% in HD), irritability, apathy, impulsivity, obsessive-compulsive behaviors, psychosis. Suicidal ideation screening is **critical** — suicide rate in HD is 4-6 times the general population, with highest risk around the time of diagnosis and in early symptomatic disease.\n- **Behavioral changes**: Disinhibition, perseveration, poor judgment, inappropriate social behavior. Wife\'s report of erratic classroom behavior and near-termination is significant.\n- **Motor function**: Gait instability, falls, tandem walking difficulty, fine motor impairment (handwriting, typing), dysphagia screening.\n- **Family history details**: Father\'s age of onset (42), disease course, cause of death. Paternal vs maternal transmission matters — paternal transmission is associated with **anticipation** (earlier onset in successive generations due to CAG repeat expansion during spermatogenesis).\n- **Functional impact**: UHDRS (Unified Huntington\'s Disease Rating Scale) — Total Functional Capacity (TFC) score, independence scale. Current ability to manage finances, drive, work, perform household tasks.\n- **Weight**: HD causes hypermetabolism. Track weight loss, caloric intake. Dysphagia screening.\n- **Children**: The 18-year-old daughter and 15-year-old son each have a 50% chance of inheriting the gene. This will become a central issue.',
