      content: '**Vitals**: BP 128/76 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 14, Weight 168 lbs (down from 178 lbs one year ago per PCP records), BMI 23.4.\n\n**General**: Appears younger than stated age. Frequent involuntary movements of the face and upper extremities throughout the examination. Restless, difficulty sitting still. Intermittent grimacing.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x4. **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA): 23/30** — deficits in trails (executive function), verbal fluency (generated only 8 words beginning with "F" in 60 seconds; normal >12), and delayed recall (2/5 words). No insight impairment currently.\n- **Motor — Chorea Assessment**: Moderate generalized chorea affecting face (grimacing, eyebrow elevation, lip pursing), bilateral upper extremities (random jerking movements of fingers, wrists, and shoulders), mild trunk involvement, and mild lower extremity involvement. Chorea worsens with conversation (dual-tasking) and when asked about his father.\n- **Tone**: Mildly reduced (hypotonia is typical in chorea-predominant HD; rigidity and bradykinesia emerge in later stages).\n- **Motor Impersistence**: Cannot maintain tongue protrusion for 10 seconds (\"milkmaid\'s grip\" also present — inability to maintain constant hand squeeze). This is characteristic of HD.\n- **Eye Movements**: Slowed saccadic initiation. Head thrusting to initiate saccades — the patient moves his head first, then eyes follow. Impaired smooth pursuit. These oculomotor findings are early and characteristic of HD.\n- **Gait**: Wide-based, irregular cadence. Choreiform movements of trunk and extremities during walking. Difficulty with tandem gait. No festination or freezing. Turns are unsteady.\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ throughout, symmetric. Toes downgoing bilaterally.\n- **Coordination**: Mild dysmetria on finger-to-nose (confounded by chorea). Impaired rapid alternating movements.\n- **Speech**: Mildly dysarthric with irregular rate and rhythm. Hypernasality not present.\n\n**Psychiatric Screen**: PHQ-9 score: 14 (moderate depression). Denies active suicidal ideation but acknowledges passive death wish: "Sometimes I think my family would be better off if I just weren\'t around." **This requires immediate further assessment.**\n\n**UHDRS Total Functional Capacity (TFC)**: 10/13 (Stage II — reduced capacity at work, reduced financial management, impaired driving).',
