    const Section(
      title: '+ Explain the genetic testing for HD including CAG repeat interpretation, and describe the HD staging system. What cognitive battery would you use?',
      content: '**Genetic Testing for Huntington Disease**:\n\n**The HD Gene (HTT gene, chromosome 4p16.3)**: HD is caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the huntingtin gene. The test is a simple blood draw that measures the number of CAG repeats.\n\n**CAG Repeat Interpretation**:\n- **<27 repeats**: Normal. Will not develop HD. Cannot pass to children.\n- **27-35 repeats (Intermediate)**: Will NOT develop HD, but the repeat can expand during transmission to offspring (especially paternal). Genetic counseling recommended.\n- **36-39 repeats (Reduced Penetrance)**: May or may not develop HD. Incomplete penetrance. Variable onset and severity. Children at risk.\n- **≥40 repeats (Full Penetrance)**: **Will develop HD** if they live long enough. 100% penetrance. Each child has 50% chance of inheriting the expanded allele.\n\n**Inverse Correlation**: Higher CAG repeat number correlates with earlier age of onset. Juvenile HD (onset <20 years) is associated with very high repeat numbers (typically >60) and usually transmitted paternally due to anticipation.\n\n**Anticipation**: The CAG repeat can expand during spermatogenesis, so paternal transmission may result in longer repeats and earlier onset in successive generations. This patient\'s father had onset at 42 — this patient\'s onset at 38 is consistent with anticipation.\n\n**Pre-test Genetic Counseling Requirements**:\nGenetic testing for HD should NEVER be ordered casually. International guidelines (HDSA) require:\n1. Pre-test genetic counseling by a certified genetic counselor\n2. Assessment of the patient\'s psychological readiness for the result\n3. Discussion of implications for employment, insurance, family members\n4. Suicide risk assessment (risk is highest around time of testing/diagnosis)\n5. A companion who can provide support\n6. A waiting period (typically 1 month between counseling and blood draw)\n7. Results delivered in person, never by phone or mail\n\n**For this patient**: Given his clinical presentation (chorea, cognitive decline, psychiatric symptoms, family history, age of onset), the diagnosis is clinically near-certain. However, genetic confirmation is still important for definitive diagnosis, family planning implications, and clinical trial eligibility.\n\n**Huntington Disease Staging (Shoulson-Fahn Staging based on TFC)**:\n- **Stage I (TFC 11-13)**: Fully functional at work and home. Able to manage finances and domestic responsibilities.\n- **Stage II (TFC 7-10)**: Functional at work but at reduced capacity. Can still handle domestic tasks but with difficulty. **This patient is Stage II (TFC 10).**\n- **Stage III (TFC 3-6)**: Unable to work. Requires assistance with finances. Can perform basic ADLs with some help.\n- **Stage IV (TFC 1-2)**: Dependent for most ADLs. Requires nursing-level care. Severe chorea or rigidity.\n- **Stage V (TFC 0)**: Total dependence. Bed-bound. Requires full nursing care.\n\n**Cognitive Assessment Battery for HD**:\n- **HD-CAB (Huntington Disease Cognitive Assessment Battery)**: Standardized battery specifically validated for HD. Includes Symbol Digit Modalities Test, Stroop Interference Test, Trail Making Test B, Paced Tapping, One Touch Stockings of Cambridge (planning), and Emotion Recognition.\n- **MoCA**: Useful for screening but not specific to HD. Executive function subtests are most relevant.\n- **Verbal Fluency Tests**: Category fluency and phonemic fluency — highly sensitive to HD-related cognitive decline.\n- **Neuropsychological testing**: Comprehensive evaluation to establish baseline and track progression. Essential for work capacity determination.',
