    const Section(
      title: '+ The patient\'s PHQ-9 is 14 and he endorses a passive death wish. How do you manage psychiatric symptoms in HD?',
      content: '**Psychiatric Emergency Management and HD Psychiatric Care**:\n\n**Immediate Suicide Risk Assessment for This Patient**:\nThe passive death wish statement ("my family would be better off without me") requires urgent evaluation:\n1. **Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS)**: Administer immediately. Distinguish passive ideation from active ideation, plan, and intent.\n2. **Risk factors specific to HD**: Period around diagnosis is highest risk. Family history of HD-related suicide. Loss of functional roles (work impairment). Depression. Impulsivity (HD-related disinhibition lowers the threshold for acting on suicidal thoughts).\n3. **Means restriction**: Ask about firearm access, medications in the home. If firearms are present, counsel on safe storage or temporary removal.\n4. **Safety plan**: Develop a written safety plan with the patient identifying warning signs, coping strategies, people to contact, and crisis hotline numbers (988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline).\n5. **If active suicidal ideation with plan or intent**: Psychiatric emergency evaluation. Do not discharge from clinic.\n\n**This patient**: He has moderate depression, passive death wish without active ideation, and is in a high-risk period (peri-diagnostic). He needs same-week psychiatry referral, safety planning, and close follow-up. He should NOT be left alone until his wife confirms she understands the risk.\n\n**Psychiatric Management in HD**:\n\n**Depression (30-50% prevalence in HD)**:\n- **First-line**: SSRIs — citalopram or sertraline are preferred. These also help with irritability and obsessive symptoms.\n- **Second-line**: Venlafaxine or mirtazapine (mirtazapine has the advantage of appetite stimulation and sedation for insomnia)\n- **Avoid**: Bupropion — may lower seizure threshold and worsen chorea. TCAs — anticholinergic burden worsens cognition.\n- **Psychotherapy**: CBT adapted for cognitive impairment. Supportive therapy. Caregiver therapy.\n\n**Irritability and Aggression (most common behavioral complaint in HD)**:\n- **First-line**: SSRIs (citalopram, sertraline)\n- **Second-line**: Low-dose atypical antipsychotics (olanzapine, quetiapine) — these also suppress chorea. Valproic acid for mood stabilization.\n- **Behavioral strategies**: Structured routines, reduced environmental stimulation, caregiver education about triggers.\n\n**Apathy (distinct from depression — most disabling psychiatric symptom in later stages)**:\n- No well-established pharmacologic treatment. SSRIs may worsen apathy. Structured activity schedules and caregiver-led engagement are the primary strategies.\n- Differentiate apathy from depression — they have different mechanisms and treatments.\n\n**Psychosis (rare but occurs in 3-11% of HD)**:\n- Atypical antipsychotics: olanzapine or quetiapine preferred. Risperidone can worsen motor symptoms.\n- Avoid typical antipsychotics (haloperidol) — they suppress chorea but cause severe rigidity and swallowing impairment.\n\n**Obsessive-Compulsive Behaviors (common in HD)**:\n- SSRIs are first-line. Higher doses may be needed than for depression.\n\n**Sleep Disturbance**:\n- Melatonin for circadian rhythm disruption. Trazodone or mirtazapine for insomnia.\n- Avoid benzodiazepines — they worsen cognitive impairment and increase fall risk.',
