    const Section(
      title: '+ Outline pharmacologic management of chorea, fall prevention, and the rehabilitation program for this patient.',
      content: '**Chorea Management**:\n\n**Tetrabenazine (Xenazine)** — First-line pharmacologic treatment for HD chorea:\n- Mechanism: Vesicular monoamine transporter 2 (VMAT2) inhibitor — depletes presynaptic dopamine\n- Starting dose: 12.5 mg daily, titrate by 12.5 mg every week. Maximum 100 mg/day (in divided doses TID).\n- **CYP2D6 testing is required before exceeding 50 mg/day** — poor metabolizers have higher drug levels and increased side effect risk\n- Side effects: Depression (BLACK BOX WARNING — contraindicated in actively suicidal patients), sedation, parkinsonism, akathisia, dysphagia\n- **For this patient**: His depression and passive suicidal ideation are a relative contraindication. Must stabilize psychiatric symptoms before initiating or use an alternative.\n\n**Deutetrabenazine (Austedo)** — Preferred alternative:\n- Deuterated form of tetrabenazine with longer half-life, more stable plasma levels, less frequent dosing (BID vs TID), and **fewer psychiatric side effects**\n- Starting dose: 6 mg daily, titrate by 6 mg weekly to a maximum of 48 mg/day (in divided doses BID)\n- Still carries a depression/suicidality warning but appears to have lower risk than tetrabenazine\n- **Better choice for this patient given his psychiatric comorbidities**\n\n**Valbenazine (Ingrezza)**: FDA-approved for tardive dyskinesia, not HD chorea, but uses the same VMAT2 mechanism. Used off-label.\n\n**Atypical Antipsychotics**: Olanzapine or risperidone suppress chorea and may be useful if psychosis or severe aggression coexists.\n\n**Important**: Not all chorea requires treatment. If chorea is mild and not functionally limiting or dangerous, observation is appropriate. Suppressing chorea with VMAT2 inhibitors can unmask underlying bradykinesia and worsen gait.\n\n**Fall Prevention Program**:\n- **PT evaluation**: Balance assessment (Berg Balance Scale, TUG), gait analysis, identify chorea-related and cognitive-related fall risk\n- **Home safety**: Remove tripping hazards, install grab bars, adequate lighting, consider handrails on both sides of stairs\n- **Footwear**: Supportive, flat, non-slip shoes. Avoid loose slippers.\n- **Gait training**: Wide-based gait strategies, safe turning techniques, dual-task avoidance during mobility\n- **Assistive devices**: May be needed as disease progresses — weighted walker can help counteract chorea-induced instability. Wheelchair assessment for community mobility when falls become frequent.\n- **Hip protectors**: For patients with recurrent falls\n- **Driving assessment**: Given cognitive decline and chorea, formal driving evaluation by a CDRS is essential. His TFC of 10 suggests driving may already be unsafe.\n\n**Comprehensive Rehabilitation Program**:\n- **Physical therapy**: Aerobic exercise (shown to improve motor function in HD), balance training, gait training with cueing, flexibility and stretching\n- **Occupational therapy**: Workplace accommodations assessment, adaptive strategies for fine motor tasks, home modification, energy conservation\n- **Speech therapy**: Dysarthria management, swallowing assessment and monitoring (dysphagia is a leading cause of death in HD from aspiration), communication strategies as disease progresses\n- **Cognitive rehabilitation**: Compensatory strategies (external memory aids, structured schedules, simplified routines), vocational rehabilitation to maximize work tenure\n- **Nutritional counseling**: High-calorie diet (HD increases metabolic demands significantly). Monitor weight at every visit — weight loss correlates with disease progression. May require 3000-4000 calories/day to maintain weight.',
