    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss employment protections (GINA, ADA), insurance implications of genetic testing, and genetic counseling referral for at-risk family members.',
      content: '**Employment and Insurance Protections**:\n\n**Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act (GINA, 2008)**:\n- **Title I**: Prohibits health insurance companies from using genetic information (including genetic test results, family history of genetic disease) to make eligibility, coverage, or premium decisions\n- **Title II**: Prohibits employers with 15+ employees from using genetic information in hiring, firing, job assignment, or promotion decisions\n- **GINA Limitations**: GINA does NOT protect against discrimination in **life insurance, disability insurance, or long-term care insurance**. This is critical — if this patient has not yet purchased these policies, he should be advised to do so BEFORE genetic testing is on his medical record.\n- **For this patient**: His employer cannot fire him based on his HD genetic status. However, if his job performance has deteriorated to the point of legitimate performance concerns (as suggested by the near-firing), the employer can act on documented performance issues.\n\n**Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)**:\n- HD qualifies as a disability under the ADA once it substantially limits major life activities\n- The employer must provide **reasonable accommodations**: reduced workload, modified schedule, additional time for grading, quiet workspace, assistance with classroom management\n- The employer can require a fitness-for-duty evaluation if there are legitimate safety or performance concerns\n- Teaching may become untenable as cognitive decline progresses — but accommodations must be attempted first\n\n**Insurance Implications**:\n- **Health insurance**: Protected by GINA and the ACA (Affordable Care Act) — cannot be denied or charged more based on pre-existing conditions including HD\n- **Life insurance**: NOT protected by GINA. If the patient does not have life insurance, genetic test results could lead to denial. **Advise the patient to obtain life insurance before genetic test results are documented.**\n- **Long-term care insurance**: NOT protected by GINA. Same advice — purchase before testing.\n- **Disability insurance**: NOT protected by GINA. Obtain before testing.\n- **COBRA**: If the patient loses employment, COBRA provides 18 months of continued health insurance coverage\n- **SSDI/Medicare**: As HD progresses, the patient will qualify for SSDI. HD is on the Compassionate Allowances list (similar to ALS) for expedited processing.\n\n**Genetic Counseling for At-Risk Family Members**:\n- The patient\'s **18-year-old daughter and 15-year-old son** each have a **50% chance** of having inherited the HD gene mutation\n- **The patient should be the one to disclose his diagnosis to his children**, with guidance from a genetic counselor on how to have this conversation\n- Predictive genetic testing in **minors is generally not recommended** by the HDSA and the American Society of Human Genetics unless there is a clinical indication (juvenile HD symptoms). The 15-year-old should NOT be tested.\n- The 18-year-old can pursue predictive testing if she chooses — but only through a formal genetic counseling protocol with psychological assessment and support (see Domain E for the dilemma this creates).\n- **Referral to HD Society of America (HDSA) Center of Excellence** for comprehensive genetic counseling, family support, and clinical trial access\n\n**HDSA Resources**:\n- HDSA Centers of Excellence — multidisciplinary HD clinics (similar to ALS clinics)\n- HDSA Social Worker — assist with disability applications, insurance navigation, caregiver support\n- HDSA Youth Alliance — support program specifically for young people affected by HD in their families',
