    const Section(
      title: '+ How do you respond to the daughter\'s request for predictive genetic testing, addressing ethical boundaries, genetic counseling requirements, and the emotional dimensions of this situation?',
      content: '**Navigating the Genetic Testing Request — Ethical and Communication Framework**:\n\nThis scenario tests multiple critical competencies: genetic counseling boundaries, patient autonomy vs beneficence, appropriate referral, and compassionate communication with a distressed young person.\n\n**My Response**:\n\n\'Thank you for calling me, and I can only imagine how frightening this is for you. Learning about your father\'s diagnosis is an enormous thing to process, and I think it shows real strength that you are thinking about this proactively.\n\nI want to be completely honest with you about what I can and cannot do.\n\n**What I cannot do**: I cannot order the genetic test for you myself. This is not because I don\'t think you deserve to know — it is because predictive testing for Huntington disease is a life-changing decision with profound implications, and there is a well-established process designed to protect people like you.\'\n\n**Explaining the Predictive Testing Protocol**:\n\n\'Here is why the process matters: People who test positive for the HD gene learn that they will develop a serious, progressive illness at some point in their life. Research shows that how someone receives this information — the preparation, the support, the counseling beforehand — makes a major difference in how they cope with the result.\n\nThe international guidelines for HD predictive testing require:\n1. Meeting with a certified genetic counselor who specializes in HD\n2. A psychological readiness assessment\n3. Discussion of what a positive or negative result would mean for your life, your relationships, your career plans, your future decisions about having children\n4. A companion or support person identified before the test\n5. A waiting period to ensure the decision is not impulsive\n6. Results delivered in person with immediate support available\n\nThis process exists because decades of experience have taught us that people who go through genetic counseling before testing cope significantly better with the results — regardless of whether the result is positive or negative.\'\n\n**Addressing the Secrecy Request**:\n\n\'Regarding not telling your parents — I understand why you might want to keep this private. You are 18, and you have the legal right to make your own medical decisions.\n\nHowever, I want to encourage you to think carefully about going through this alone. Your parents are facing their own grief right now, but your father may actually want to be part of supporting you. And your mother could be an important source of strength.\n\nThat said, this is ultimately your decision. A genetic counselor can help you think through whether and how to involve your family. They will respect your confidentiality.\'\n\n**Addressing the Urgency**:\n\n\'I hear that you feel you need to know before starting college. I understand the desire for certainty. But I want you to know that there is no medical urgency here. If you carry the gene, it would likely be decades before symptoms would appear, given that your father\'s symptoms started at 38.\n\nSome people in your situation choose to get tested because not knowing feels unbearable. Others choose not to be tested — they prefer to live without that knowledge. Both choices are valid. A genetic counselor can help you explore which path is right for you, right now.\'\n\n**Concrete Next Steps**:\n\n\'What I can do for you today is refer you to a genetic counselor at the HDSA Center of Excellence nearest to your college. They will see you confidentially. They will not contact your parents unless you give permission. And they will walk you through this process at your pace.\n\nI am also going to give you the HDSA helpline number and their youth program — there are other young adults who have been exactly where you are now, and many of them have found it incredibly helpful to connect with peers.\n\nCan I set that referral up for you?\'\n\n**Key Ethical Principles Demonstrated**:\n- **Autonomy**: Respecting her legal right to make medical decisions at 18\n- **Beneficence**: Ensuring she has proper support and counseling before testing\n- **Non-maleficence**: Protecting her from potential harm of receiving devastating results without adequate psychological preparation\n- **Scope of practice**: Recognizing that ordering predictive genetic tests without genetic counseling infrastructure is below the standard of care\n- **Appropriate referral**: Connecting her with the right specialist rather than exceeding your expertise\n- **Emotional attunement**: Validating her fear while providing structure and hope',
